<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.1.2: Recognizing Structure in Perfect-Square Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02333</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.1.2: Recognizing Structure in Perfect-Square Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>acc10f7e-6d2f-4dd2-a1d4-a709485e0549</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
        <para>1. Each expression is written as the product of factors. Write an equivalent expression in standard form.</para>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\((3x)^2\)</para>
          <para>Write down your answer, then select the<emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(9x^2\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\(7x \cdot 7x\)</para>
          <para>Write down your answer, then select the<emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(49x^2\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\((x+4)(x+4)\)</para>
          <para>Write down your answer, then select the<emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(x^2+8x+16\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\((x+1)^2\)</para>
          <para>Write down your answer, then select the<emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(x^2+2x+1\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\((x−7)^2\)</para>
          <para>Write down your answer, then select the<emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(x^2−14x+49\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\((x+n)^2\)</para>
          <para>Write down your answer, then select the<emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(x^2+2nx+n^2\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
        <para>2. Why do you think the following expressions can be described as <term class="glossary-tooltip">perfect square</term>s?</para>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\(x^2+6x+9\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;x^2−16x+64\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;x^2+ \frac {1}{3}x+ \frac {1}{36}\)</para>
          <para>Write down your answer, then select the<emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>If these expressions are written in factored form, each one is the square of a linear expression:</para>
          <para>\((x+3)^2\), \((x−8)^2\), and \((x+\frac {1}{6})^2\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <section>
        <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
        <section>
          <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
          <exercise id="_98bb2177-78bd-418f-8bce-0281b92105e1">
            <problem>
              <para>Write each expression in factored form.</para>
              <para>\(x^4 - 30x^2 + 225\)</para>
              <para>Write down your answer, then select the<emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution>
              <para>Compare your answer:</para>
              <para>\((x^2 - 15)^2\)</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
          <!-- End CTA block -->
          <exercise id="_98f46003-e34f-4b77-8941-de4c32937d47">
            <problem>
              <para>\(x + 14\sqrt{x} + 49\)</para>
              <para>Write down your answer, then select the<emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution>
              <para>Compare your answer:</para>
              <para>\((\sqrt{x} + 7)^2\)</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
          <!-- End CTA block -->
          <exercise id="_37387496-60e5-4afe-95cc-4ab2cfee8365">
            <problem>
              <para>\(5^{2x} + 6 \cdot 5^x + 9\)</para>
              <para>Write down your answer, then select the<emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution>
              <para>Compare your answer:</para>
              <para>\((5^x + 3)^2\)</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
          <!-- End CTA block -->
        </section>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>